SECOND MAXIMALITY CONSULT — **one further material scope directive should block D0.5.**

The present \(402\) result is maximal for its fixed coordinates, but the proof actually yields a contribution-level contrast-support phase transition for every fixed scalar rank-one contrast, including arbitrary \(K\). This is not local constant polishing.

For \(c\in\mathbb R^K\), let \(r=|\operatorname{supp}(c)|\) and define the analogous same-\(N\) gap \(\Delta_{N,K,c}\).

- If \(r\le2\), then \(\Delta_{N,K,c}=0\): inactive arms are irrelevant and the active one- or two-marginal transportation polytope is integral.

- If \(r\ge3\), then \(\Delta_{N,K,c}=\Theta_c(N^{-1})\). For each active arm set \[ H_a=|c_a|+2\sum_{b\ne a}|c_b| =2\|c\|_1-|c_a|. \] The existing slope proof places \(I_{as}\) in an interval of length \(H_a\). With \(D_\ell=2^\ell\), the \(r\)-arm incidence matrix has \(r-1\) row dependencies, giving \[ R_{\ell+1}\le \sum_aK_{a,\ell+1}-r \le 2r(2^{\ell+1}-1). \] The quadratic identity now uses \[ \Big(\sum_a\delta_a\Big)^2-\sum_a\delta_a^2 \le(r-1)\sum_a\delta_a^2, \] so each moved coordinate costs at most \(r h_{a,\ell}^2\). Consequently \[ \Delta_{N,K,c} <\frac{16r^2}{3N}\sum_{a\in\operatorname{supp}(c)}H_a^2. \] For the current \(r=3\), \(c=(1/4,1/4,-1/2)\), this specializes exactly to \(402/N\).

For the converse, choose any three nonzero coefficients and affine-place the existing unshifted obstruction coordinates inside the segments between \(0\) and those coefficients; make every other arm constant. With the displayed obstruction spans \(109,116,207\), one may take \[ \lambda_c=\min\{|c_{a_1}|/109,\ |c_{a_2}|/116,\ |c_{a_3}|/207\}>0, \] and the gap is \(5\lambda_c^2/N\). Thus the \(N^{-1}\) rate holds exactly when at least three coefficients are active. For the current contrast this also gives the incidental sharper lower constant \(5/215296\), but the material advance is the full support-size dichotomy and arbitrary-\(K\) transfer.

